      "writtenOn": "2024-11-14T15:49:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "No problem, the distinction is the other cases disable it only if the sanitizer is implicitly enabled via another sanitizer (e.g. `fsanitize\u003dinteger`), but leave it enabled if explicitly requested.\n\nWe should only disable `function` if it\u0027s not explicitly requested in  the `Misc_undefined` property, such as if its enabled via `undefined` (which becomes `fsanitize\u003dundefined`). If `misc_undefined` contains either `function` or `kcfi` and XOM is enabled, we should throw an error as they\u0027re incompatible and the developer should make an explicit decision about which to remove.\n\nI\u0027d suggest adding the check in `sanitize.begin()` alongside some of the other checks -- if XOM is enabled and either of these sanitizers are defined in `misc_undefined`, then throw a ctx.ModuleErrorf() error.",
